          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What counts as insomnia rather than just a few bad nights?",
          "answer": "Insomnia is defined by difficulty falling or staying asleep, or waking earlier than intended, despite having enough opportunity to sleep. A key part of the definition is that these sleep problems also lead to noticeable problems during the day, such as fatigue or difficulty concentrating. Without these daytime effects, short sleep is not considered a disorder (Source: General clinical reference, educational summary; Sleep medicine society and internal medicine college guidance, educational summary).\n\nTemporary sleep disturbances, sometimes called short-term insomnia, last for days to weeks and usually have a clear cause. These often resolve on their own. In contrast, chronic insomnia disorder is diagnosed when these sleep difficulties occur at least three nights per week for at least three months, and persist even after any initial cause has passed (Source: General clinical reference, educational summary; Sleep medicine society and internal medicine college guidance, educational summary).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
